A few notes on judgment calls, since the exercise resolves disagreements against sources:

- **Developer vs operator.** For labs running their own evaluations (OpenAI, Anthropic, Meta, Google), I coded the disclosing/notifying role as `developer` throughout, since the model-maker is the recognizable actor. A coder who reads these as `operator` (party that ran the agent) would diverge on every lab-eval row.
- **The three Anthropic/Irregular rows (1627, 1628, 1629).** I left `event_date` unknown because the source only says "the earliest incidents date to April" without dating each incident, and set `first_sign_date` to the 2026-07-27 notification. For 1629 I flagged that one of the three orgs was never reached, so its awareness is genuinely uncertain.
- **Harmed-party-knew vs intrusion-detected.** For 1604 (Hugging Face) I treated detection/containment as the first sign; for 1633 and 1649 I coded `first_sign` unknown because the targeted humans/sites are shown reacting to malware or a breach, not to knowledge that an AI agent was behind it.
- **1563 (KPMG)** has `first_sign` unknown deliberately: AI involvement is stated to be unconfirmed, so no dated moment where the harmed firms knew an AI agent was involved, even though they could name KPMG as a responsible party.